mcp-hub-tools
=============

Hub-side tools for the server-mcp-gemini project's Windows-PC tunnel
fleet. Installs:

  /usr/bin/hub-cli          -- manage PC tunnel config, pin SSH host
                                keys, read/write the Render-hosted
                                service's env vars
  /usr/bin/hub-diagnostics  -- local health checks (tunnel services,
                                port reachability, Tailscale, disk space)
  /lib/systemd/system/pc-tunnel@.service
                             -- the systemd template each configured PC
                                gets an instance of

Quick start
-----------
Add a new PC:

    sudo hub-cli pc add desktop 192.168.1.50
    sudo hub-cli pc pin desktop
    sudo hub-cli render set PCS '{"desktop": {"port": 7842, "secret": "..."}}'

Check everything's healthy:

    hub-diagnostics

See `hub-cli --help` / `hub-diagnostics --help` for the full command
list.

Tailscale SSH ACL gotcha
-------------------------
If the hub reaches your home server over Tailscale SSH (rather than a
plain key-based SSH the hub already trusts), Tailscale's DEFAULT ACL
action for SSH is "check", which requires interactive approval in a
browser the first time a connection is made. In an automated context
(this project's Render-hosted MCP server calling in on a schedule, or
this package's own autoupdate timer, or anything else non-interactive)
that "check" just hangs for about 60 seconds and then fails -- there's
no browser to approve it in.

Fix: in your Tailscale ACL policy, change the relevant ssh rule's
action from "check" to "accept" for this use case:

    "ssh": [
      {
        "action": "accept",
        "src": ["..."],
        "dst": ["..."],
        "users": ["..."]
      }
    ]

This was hit for real during this project's first live end-to-end
verification (MCP client -> Render -> Tailscale -> home server -> real
command execution) -- worth doing this BEFORE your first automated run,
not after watching it hang once.

Auto-update
------------
A systemd timer (`mcp-hub-tools-autoupdate.timer`) can check GitHub
Releases daily for a newer version of this package and install it
automatically. It is DISABLED by default -- installing this package
does not opt you into automatic self-modification.

To enable it:

    sudo systemctl enable --now mcp-hub-tools-autoupdate.timer

What it actually does (read this before enabling): once a day (plus up
to a 1-hour random delay, to avoid every install hitting GitHub's API
at exactly the same moment), it checks the latest GitHub Release for
this repo, and if the tag is newer than what's installed, downloads the
.deb asset, verifies it against the SHA256 checksum GitHub publishes
alongside it, and runs `dpkg -i`.

Trust model, stated plainly: this authenticates the download CHANNEL
(HTTPS to github.com, checksum-verified against what GitHub itself
published) but the .deb is not GPG-signed. This does not protect
against a compromised GitHub account or repo pushing a malicious
release -- the same trust model as most small projects'
`curl | bash`-style installers, made explicit here rather than implied.
If that trade-off doesn't work for your threat model, leave the timer
disabled and update manually (`sudo dpkg -i <downloaded .deb>`) after
reviewing each release yourself.

Uninstalling
-------------
`apt remove mcp-hub-tools` stops the autoupdate timer but keeps your
`/etc/pc-tunnel/` config (conf files + pinned host keys) in place, in
case you're reinstalling rather than actually decommissioning.

`apt purge mcp-hub-tools` removes that config too. If any
`pc-tunnel@<name>.service` instances are still active when you
remove/purge, you'll get a warning listing them (their tunnels will
stop working once the template unit file is gone) -- it doesn't block
removal, just tells you.
